The Scenario:

A friend of mine and I were brainstorming last night on nothing in particular 
when we started talking about the new VAXStation 4000 Model 60. For about      
$9,000 (list), the machine comes with VMS 5.5, built-in Ethernet, lots (104
Megs) of RAM, and an 852-Meg drive. A nice system, to say the least.

Anyway, we started to extrapolate on the possibilities. Say (for the sake
of the argument) you wanted to put 7 of the 4000/60s in a VAXCluster 
in order to create a 'virtual' vax; as users log in to 'virtual', they're
pawned off on the 4000/60 with the smallest load at that time. 

Now, each 'virtual' subpart contains VMS5.5 on the local disk, and 
frequently used executables, etc. -- only limited by the amount of disk
space on the 'virtual' subpart. The (in this case) 7 4000/60s are connected
via Ethernet to each other, and subsequently, to a bridge and out to the
world. 

But the 'virtual' parts need access to a commons area for diskstorage,
accounts, etc. To accomplish this, we put each 'virtual' part -- the client - 
on two (2) networks -- the Ethernet/VAXcluster connecting network and a 
separate, self-contained network (in the illustration below, Ethernet) for
disk access. The server, in this case, would be an identical 4000/60 with
a chain of scsi devices off it, or perhaps some TurboChannel-based system.
	
The Model:
			       DELNI
			.__________________.
		        | \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  |
		        `-|-|-|-|-|-|-|-|--'		In this model, both
			 / / /  | |  \ \ \	        the clients (C) and the
		       / / /    | |    \ \ \		server (S) are complete	
		     / / /      | |      \ \ \	        VAXStation 4000 Model
VAXClustered	    C C C       C C       C C S  	60s with VMS 5.5 and
behind the bridge   | | |       | |       | | 		104 Megs of RAM.
	            | | |       | |       | |
  	       |----+-+-+---+---+-+-------+-+----|		
			    |
			    |	
			   / \ Bridge
			   \ /	
			    |
                  |---------+---------------|

The Problems:

	1) Besides FDDI, what networking options can take advantage of
	   the TurboChannel bus? While the 4000/60s have built-in Ethernet
	   capabilities, they only have one. They're limited in their
	   VAXCluster support to Ethernet only, and while FDDI would be an
           incredible speed offering, the TurboChannel bus is limited to
	   50 Mbit/second.

 	2) What drive ctlrs support TurboChannel? The server would only need
	   to be on one of the networks (as a straight disk controller), so
	   its TC bus would be open; TC cards have a theoretical I/O
	   10 times faster than SCSI.

	3) Will it even work? (No flames, please -- just constructive
	   criticism. Remember, this was brainstorming.)

The Advantages:

If (and that's a big "if") the above would work, IMHO the advantages would
be tremendous. Each 4000/60 has a 12-VUP rating, so each 'virtual' part of
the cluster would have the relative computing power of VAX 6000 Model 510.
In theory, a set of seven 4000/60s operating as a 'virtual' VAX would have
a relative (the operative word here) computing power of a lower-range VAX
9000 -- approximately 80-90 VUPs. In a slight stretch of the term, this
'virtual' machine would be multiprocessing -- each part capable of handling
multiple jobs (via swapping) -- and load levelling, with every machine (in 
theory) having the same load average over time (users log in, are sent to 
machines with 'less' load, etc).
But perhaps the biggest advantage would be cost. At list price, you could
set up a system like this (hardware only) for approximately $13,000 per
part -- and that's with an FDDI card for the disknet. Multiply that by
eight (seven clients and one server), and for a little more than $100,000
you have the 'relative' computing power of a VAX 9000 for approximately
1/10th the cost.

Anyway, food for thought. Please direct comments comp.sys.dec
or directly to me via e-mail, and I'll post the responses.

Dave Manning
fg040@unocss.unomaha.edu


