First as I recall, the turbochannel is 50 MByte/sec not bit, therefore more
than enough to support the fddi.

But my question is do you need that much network bandwidth?  Please read on.

> 	3) Will it even work? (No flames, please -- just constructive
> 	   criticism. Remember, this was brainstorming.)

Yes it will work.  I've had almost this arrangement for 3 years.  Except it
centers on 8 VS3200's and 2 VS3100's.  Not quite the 84 vups of the 4000's
but good for three years ago, and I'll be adding 4000's as soon as I can get
a few bucks together.  The system is not quite virtual, in that the user's
decide which VAX to log into.  We have some rules on who goes where, but the
users basically load balance themselves, and do so quite well.  Our system
is configured as a LAVC, with 3 boot nodes (there are some VSII systems also).
The I only consume VMS on those disks.  All but one system has a local drive,
total disk space between scsi and esdi drives is about 7 Gbytes.  Some local
drives have user accounts and some have executables for performance, but most
accounts and software come only a few disks.  We use batch system for running
image reconstructions in parallel with data that is local on each computer.
data sets are in excess of 25 megs per system, and we process up to 20 data
sets per data, roughly 1-2 Gig a day.  Half the systems are running motif, half
decwindows.  It is not unusual to see over 100 user processes.  I also use ram
disk on most systems that need fast i/o from file.  I use just the simple
dec one.  User response is good, 2 systems do real time data acquistions at up
to 800Kbytes per second.  I use a vaxstation II at my desk, and run  motif
from another system (I'll admit logon is bear).  

Now some points to bear in mind:  The 3200's have the slow ethernet controller,
(as compared to the 4000's, 3-4Mbits vs 8+Mbits throughput). I have between 
16 and 24 Mbytes of memory - not excessive amount.  I have ALL disks shared to
everyone through LAVC.  I have VMS only on 3 disks.  I process over 1-2 Gbytes
of new data each day, plus alot of other research.  Plus windows, plus
programming, compiling, linking.  A network analyzer shows our ethernet load to
be less than 10 percent with occaisional peaks to 30-40 percent, but it 
usually at 6-7%. Over LAVC and block i/o, I get 300-400 Kbytes/sec (those 30-40
percent peaks.  This is why I ask you why you need FDDI? I'm not saying it
wouldn't be nice,  but unless you have an LAVC w/o local paging disks, and your
not moving images from one computer to another for display, your ethernet load
is not going to be your limiting factor.  Just balance the applications and
users to local drives, use a small amount of RAM disks.

Is this the best system - No - we have to manage multiple system disks, a DSSI
and shared disks/boot nodes would ease system management - don't try and manage
7 or more VMS's.  More memory helps.  BYTW we have several suns and decstations
sharing disks and tape through NFS on these VAXES too.  You point to several
advantages - Cheap CPU cycles running VMS.  If your applications can be run in
parallel, than the system can get alot of cycles.  As I pointed out before, let
the users load balance or if many users are regular and on at similar times,
assign them specific nodes.

Gary Muswick


