In article <1991Nov15.100800.884@news.cs.indiana.edu>,
vogt@iuvax.cs.indiana.edu (Michael Vogt) writes:
|>
|>    Does anyone know if the number of line on a page, when using
|>    the cms annotate (actually any command) command, can be modified?
|>
|>    The scenerio is this.  We are using a form for our annotate file
|>    that allows 90 lines, so whenever we print an annotate file cms
|>    assumes we are using 66 lines per page, thus it is leaving about 1/3
|>    of the page blank.  We would like to use all 90 lines.  Any
|>    suggestions (short of editing the cms created files)?

Utilities that produce listing files should call the RTL routine
LIB$LP_LINES to determine the number of lines to print on a page.  This
routine translates logical name SYS$LP_LINES to determine that number.
If the translation of SYS$LP_LINES is a valid integer number in not less
than 30 or greater than 255, LIB$LP_LINES returns this value as the number
of lines to be put on each page.  Otherwise, it uses the default value of 66.
Thus, the following DCL command should set things up the way you want:

	$ DEFINE SYS$LP_LINES 90

Then run CMS.

--PSW


