In article <30444@nntpd.lkg.dec.com> kendrix_j@mims.enet.dec.com (John R. Kendrix) writes:
>...  What I want to do is to set up a
>queue on node A:: which the application on node B:: can print to and visa
>versa.  The two sites can talk with each other using lat and decnet. 

John,

	Off hand here are the ways,

	1) a company call Digital Equipment Corparation makes a product called
	   DECprint that will do the job,  they also had a product called
	   something like Remote Print Manager that would also do the job.

	2) instead of printing to the queues, if you COPY to the spooled 
	   devices you can do it without any additional software.  

	   For example let, on each node, the print device be LPA0:.  We would
	   then define the following logical names on each node:
		NET$PRINTER_NODE_A
		NET$PRINTER_NODE_B

	   On node A the definitions would be:
		NET$PRINTER_NODE_A	LPA0:
		NET$PRINTER_NODE_B	B::LPA0:

	   On node B the definitions would be:
		NET$PRINTER_NODE_A	A::LPA0:
		NET$PRINTER_NODE_B	LPA0:

	   The application would then COPY (also could TYPE, or write) to the
	   logical NET$PRINTER_NODE_x: as needed without needing to know where
	   they are running.

