Gentlepeople,

I'm having a bit of trouble formatting Hard Disks on DEC Professional 350s.
I'm not sure if I am fighting a software problem and/or a hardware problem.


Background.
===========

A friend of a friend rescued roughly a dozen PRO 350s and 380s before they
went to the tip.  All the hard disks were ratted, there are not enough
monitors and keyboards and the RX50s were in poor condition.

I've ended up with one PRO 380 and eight PRO 350s.  I've cannibalised some
Rainbow 100s for parts, started putting together some 350s and lining up
good homes for them.  I've got two PRO 350s at home right now.

A few months ago, I got the PRO 380 working and I formatted an RD50 under
RT-11 V5.2, but I've loaned it to a friend at a hospital who is dragging
data off an RD51 from a dead PRO 380, so I can't compare it with the PRO
350s.

I have an RD50 (ST506, 5 Mbyte), an RD51 (ST412, 10 Mbyte), an RD52
(Quantum 540, 30 Mbyte) and an RD31 (ST225, 20 Mbyte). They work well on a
Rainbow 100+ (MSDOS 3.1B and the WUTIL program. They work well on an
LSI-11/73 with a Webster SRQD11-A Controller.

Actually, the latter controller didn't work well at first with the RD50 and
RD51 as it cheats by using TTL drivers instead of Open Collector drivers.
The RD50 drive had links in selecting it as drives 1, 2, 3 and 4. The RD51
drive had links in selecting it as drives 1, 2 and 3. Thus when the SRQD11
selected drive 1, it had to drive 3 or 4 times the normal length of wire,
of which it failed miserably.  When the extra links were removed (lifted
the legs of the shorting block), the SRQD11 was happy.

Because the RD50, RD51 and RD31 all have four heads, I believe the extra
drive links are used to determine which drive type is attached to the
controller.  Can anyone confirm this?


The Problem
===========

First off, the controllers sometimes comes up bad on Power up.  Standard
diagram, slot 1 highlighted, etc.  It hasn't happened lately, though.

I've been using the FORMAT command under RT-11 V5.2 and RT-11 V5.5.  V5.2
tries mightily.  V5.5 traps to location 4.

I've tried the Install option of POS V1.7 and POS V2.0.  POS v1.7 no joy.
Last night, under POS V2.0, the RD52 formatted so I aborted the Install and
fired up RT-11 V5.5. The drive INITialised ok but a bad block scan fails
with "DUP-F-Too many bad blocks". I've created test files on it but I ran
out of time to completely fill the disk.

I haven't tried every disk with every combination of software due to lack
of time.  I have a bad habit of needing sleep. :-)   A DEC Engineer friend 
looked up a database or two and found that there are two part numbers for
the RDC50:          54-15134-00 for RD50 and RD51
                    54-15134-01 for RD31, RD32, RD52 and RD53

It did not say how to distinguish between the different parts.  Below are
the ROMs on the two controllers on hand.

           |                     |
           |                     |
           |H                    |
           |A       ----         |        RDC50 Controller ROMs
           |N       |3 |         |
           |D       |0 |   [] R3 |        R3 marked "073B2"
           |L       |0 |   [] R2 |        R2 marked "072B2"
           |E       ----   R1 [] |        R1 marked "071B2"
           -|---------------------
            L

At work, I managed to read the ROMS on two PRO 350s with RD31s, and two PRO
380s with RD52s. One of the PRO 380s has the same ROM numbers, the other
PRO 380s has R3 marked "014B2", R2 marked "013B2" and R3 marked "021B2".
One PRO 350 has the same ROM numbers as the latter PRO 380.  The other has 
R3 marked "014B2", R2 marked "013B2" and R3 marked "063B2".
 

Finally the questions
=====================

A/ Does anyone know how to distinguish between the different parts?

B/ Can the board for RD31, etc. still use the RD50 and RD51 disks?

C/ The RT-11 V5.4 manual says DW is formattable.  
   Is this still true under RT-11 V5.5?


Sorry about such a long message, thanks for any assistance.

Andy Stewart, Mad Hobbyist and DECaholic.


