In article <1991Nov6.175753.333@idiws1.idi.battelle.org>, newsmgr@idiws1.idi.battelle.org writes:
> Hello, we are looking at buying a RWZ01 rewriteable optical disk drive. We have
> a few questions about it.
> 
> 1. Is all 597 mb (both sides) seen as 1 device or does it configure as two
> devices of one side of capacity each?

The RWZ01 is a repackaged Sony device.  The Sony is reliable, but SLOW.  Maxtor
Tahiti-II drives offer faster access, higher density, and are fully compatible
with Sony media should you need to interchange drives.  But on to your
question.

There is only a single read/write head in the drive due to the current
technology, so you see a single device with about 300 MB of space on it.  You
access the other side by dismounting the cartridge, physically turning it over,
and mounting it.

> 
> 2. Is it truly infinitely rewriteable?
> 

Yes, it is.  I've been developing Archival management systems using MO drives
and jukeboxes for the last two years with no I/O failures due to rewrite
problems.  The Sony drives have, in general, proven to be extremely reliable
and rugged.

> Any hints or kinks from any users out there?

Depends on what you need.  If you are interested in archival storage, you have
to be aware of the difference between glass and plastic media.  Glass is the
media of choice if you need to store things for 25 years.  Otherwise plastic is
fine.  The Sony drives are slow enough to make booting VMS and running from
them too painful to be tolerated.  If you need to do this, consider going with
a faster seek and transfer time drive (the Maxtor is the current front runner).

