I think we got it right this time!

I would like to announce, to any interested parties, the availability of
Version 4.4.2 of the uSystem (pronounced micro-system), a library of C
routines that provide light-weight concurrency on uniprocessor and
multiprocessor computers running the UNIX operating system.

Version 4.4.2 has bug fixes from version 4.4.1, which was made available on
Oct 17, 1991.


Fixes:

1. Fixed more bugs in the non-blocking I/O routines introduced by the last
set of fixes in the non-blocking I/O routines. I think we now have a scheme
that runs solid (famous last words).

2. Added errno as part of the context of a coroutine and task.

Note:

1. For Sequent users, not all UNIX routines have shared-memory versions. As a
result, data returned from them cannot be passed to uSystem routines, which
require shared memory only.

2. We discovered that raw I/O cannot be done nonblocking.


Version 4.4.2 of the uSystem can be obtained by anonymous ftp from the
following location:

   watmsg.waterloo.edu (129.97.141.9)   pub/uSystem/uSystem.tar.Z

(Note the new IP address for watmsg.)  The distribution file is in
compressed tar format. Execute the following command to unpack the source:

   % zcat uSystem.tar.Z | tar -xf -

The README file contains all the installation instructions. Good luck and
happy concurrency.

A postscript version of the uSystem manual is available in the same ftp
directory as the uSystem, called uSystem.ps.  It contains a copy of the
uSystem documentation that has been formatted for 8.5x11 paper using LaTeX
and then converted to postscript.  This has been made available because
some people have had problems LaTeXing the documentation that comes with
the uSystem distribution. Normally, it is unnecessary to retrieve this
file as the uSystem comes with the reference manual.


The uSystem runs on the following processors: M68000 series, NS32000
series, VAX, MIPS, Intel 386, Sparc, and the following UNIX operating
systems:

- generic BSD 4.{2,3}
- UNIX System V that has BSD system calls "setitimer" and a "sigcontext"
  passed to signal handlers which contains the location of the interrupted
  program
- Apollo SR10 BSD
- Sun OS 4.x
- Tahoe BSD 4.3
- Ultrix 3.x/4.x
- DYNIX
- Umax 4.3
- IRIX 3.3

The uSystem requires at least GNU C 1.37.1. For the Encore and Sequent
Balance, GNU C version 1.37.1 must be patched with the supplied patches.
The uSystem will NOT compile using other compilers. (Don't even think about
it!)

There is a Software-Practice and Experience paper about version 4.1 of the
uSystem in the Sept 1990 issue.

The uSystem is being used to build a highly concurrent post-relational
database here at Waterloo. It is also being used for teaching purposes here
and at:

University of Sydney, Sydney, Australia,
	contact Michael Wise, michaelw@cs.su.oz.au

Algemene Hogeschool Amsterdam, Amsterdam, The Netherlands,
	contact Andre v.d. Vlies, andre@maestro.htsa.aha.nl


Except for small additions and bug fixes, development work on the uSystem
is largely frozen.  Currently, all our efforts are directed towards uC++, a
concurrent dialect of C++. uC++ supports all the features of the uSystem
but all communication is statically type-safe. uC++ is running locally and
is being tested this term in an undergraduate course. We hope to have a
version for general release at the beginning of '92. For those interested
in uC++, a postscript file containing the preliminary uC++ reference manual
is available in the same directory as the uSystem, called uC++.ps.


