Here's the synopsis of answers to the question about how to boot a DSSI
drive from a MicroVAX II. In my case, DUC0: was the right device. The
allocation class, however, doesn't seem to matter if you aren't in a
cluster (I had no trouble, that is).

My thanks to the respondents; this is what the news net is all about.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
From: Aaron Leonard <LEONARD@Arizona.edu>

I don't know whether you can or cannot boot a MVII off of
a DSSI disk.  However, I do know that you won't be able to
boot any VAX off a DSSI disk unless that VAX and that
disk have the same ALLOCLASS.  Are both your MVII and
your RF72 set to have the same ALLOCLASS?
------------------------------------------------------------------------
From: murphy@decuac.DEC.COM (Rick Murphy)

First, this is a guess, but it may get you going..
Since you've got two MSCP controllers already, try 'B DUC0'
	-Rick
------------------------------------------------------------------------
From: "Terry Kennedy, Operations Mgr" 
            <TERRY%SPCVXA.BITNET@CU.NIH.GOV>

  If it's at the 3rd MSCP position (which seems likely according to your
description), then it should boot with B DUC0. All MSCP devices are "DU"
to the boot ROMs, even though the VMS names differ. I believe that the
KFSQA looks like N MSCP controllers, one for each attached drive, and it
would then use DUD0, DUE0, etc. for additional disks.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
From: harbre@thijssen.nl (Harald v Breederode)

Here lies your mistake. A Rf type on a KFQSA is seen by the console
software as it was a DU device. So if you try to boot from DI?? things
should work. Once VMS is up and running the disk will show up as DI??.
