In article <1991Nov1.161502.2993@acsu.buffalo.edu>, kalisiak@acsu.buffalo.edu (christophe m kalisiak) writes:
> 
> I just found a TK50 with Ultrix-32M V1.2, from '86, and I was wondering
> if it would be a waste of my time to put it on my MicroVAX-II /
> VAXstation-II. What can anyone tell me about that version?

	I looked through my collection of old SPDs and couldn't
	find one for ULTRIX-32m V1.2.  I did find one for ULTRIX
	32 V1.2 and 32m V1.1 though...

	Some of the software features.

	o  Our first round of providing System V source code
	   compatibility.

	o  Some 4.3BSD kernel enhancements.  Unfortunately it
	   doesn't say which enhancements those were.

	Some features not available yet.

	o  Dynamic BBR.  Since you probably have a system with the
	   RQDX3 and RD5x disks this doesn't matter as much since
	   that controller did BBR.  If you have a controller that
	   expects the host to do BBR then you won't get that out
	   of V1.2 (KDA50 for example).

	o  No NFS.

	o  No Generic file system.

	o  No Yellow pages.

	o  No IBM 2780/378 RJE support.

	I'm uncertain if the following hardware was supported.

	o  KDA50 and available DSA disks of the time (RA81, RA60,
	   RA80).  If the KDA50 was supported I'm pretty sure the
	   RA82 wasn't supported.  It was a 600 MB disk that broke
	   the 512 MB file system size limit at the time.

	o  DHQ11 - 8 line asyncronous serial communctions controller.

	Whether it is a waste of time depends on what you expect
	of that version.  I vaguely remember it being reasonably
	stable.  It lived in a vastly simpler world and as a result
	was small compared to today's systems that support BIND,
	YP, Hesiod, NFS, X11, etc.
> 
> Thanks,
> Chris Kalisiak


-- 
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com


