> I have heard people mention a VAXstation II/RC.  How does that VAXstation
> differ from say a VAXstation II/GPX?

Ah yes, the II/RC.

There are two differences between a VAXstation II/RC and a VAXstation II:

(a) The front panel says "VAXstation II/RC".

(b) Three out of eight slots are full of glue.

The idea was that a VAXstation could be sold cheaply to universities etc
(I haven't seen a II/RC sold to anything other than a university, but I
may be wrong).  The five slots are enough room to put in a CPU, one or
two memory boards, VCB01 graphics adapter, an RQDX3 and a TK50 and/or
DEQNA (drop a memory board if you want both).

Of course Marketing and Field Service have never talked to each other,
so it was possible to buy a VAXstation II/RC, then go to FS, buy a new
backplane *and* pay FS to install it, for much less than the cost of a
new VAXstation II.  

A VAXstation II/GPX is differs from a VAXstation II in that it has a
VCB02 rather than a VCB01 as its graphics adapter.  The VCB02 has an
onboard coprocessor and extra local memory, has colour (or greyscales)
etc, whereas the VCB01 in a II (or II/RC, since the VCB is a two or 
three board set and won't fit into a /RC plane) is fairly dumb (read 
slow) and monochrome only.

I acquired a VAXstation II/RC (oddly, with a standard VAXstation II name
plate) and converted it to a proper II by swapping the backplane with
the one in my pdp11/73 -- I now own a "MicroPDP-11/RC.  8-)  

