THE PROBLEM: 
I need to run an application on both VAX/VMS and UNIX (both ULTRIX and 
third-party UNIX systems). I would like to share disks in between  VMS and 
UNIX, and know about the various NFS implementations. We are currently 
running UCX and export VMS disks to UNIX (i.e., no UCX container). The net 
data transfer is poor and far less than expected if compared with NFS 
transfer rates between two UNIX systems. Furthermore, one VMS processor can 
easily be nailed down doing NFS, which is not acceptable for production-work 
traffic.
THE APPLICATION: 
The data to be transported are several flat files of about 250 MB, being 
read-accessed with operating-system specific index files (i.e., a pointer 
to the record of interest). The usual application needs to transfer all 
data of one file to a particular CPU and calculate some comparisons with a 
query there. The application is written in FORTRAN and uses operating-system
specific access routines to be fast. Depending on the complexity of the 
query, even local computation on a VAX88xx processor with CI cluster disks 
is I/O bound. Good results have been achieved within UNIX NFS, where up 
to 8 nfs/biod processes caused a 10% load, and resulted in relatively 
little overhead.  
THE QUESTION: 
I am aware of the following alternatives: 
1) Buy both VMS and UNIX disks and update the data on both systems if needed. 
2) Move to another NFS Server application. 
3) Switch gears and buy a VMS NFS Client. 
Whereas (1) is obviously the easiest to go for, (2) and (3) would be 
attractive to test if the performance is an order of magnitude better than 
UCX. Despite the known weakness of UCX I am not sure whether the other 
products are THAT much better in performance. 

Any experience, performance or  benchmark data , etc. highly appreciated. 


Regards 
