In article <1991Oct28.221525.12562@cass.ma02.bull.com> nstern@cass.ma02.bull.com (Neil Stern) writes:
>Does the VT320 terminal support its own emulator, or functionality
>as a super-set of the VT220 terminal or is it just a newer VT220.
>In other words, is there such a thing as an application that
>requires vt320 emulation or support that won't work with a VT220
>emulator or terminal?

--

Yes.  The VT320 does support some additional features (status line, for one)
beyond those of the VT220.  It has a distinct identity that CAN be checked
for by application software, though I don't know if this is ever done.  I
know that DECforms allows this type of selection, for example; you can tell
your application to REQUIRE VT3xx or above, for example.  In practice, this
type of thing is probably rare.

BTW, the VT420 has quite a few additional capabilities beyond the VT320's,
notably rectangular operations and off-screen memory.

