In article <1991Oct31.141502.137@uwovax.uwo.ca>, brent@uwovax.uwo.ca 
(Brent Sterner) writes:
>    Computing Canada printed an article Oct 10, 1991, page 54, referencing
> QIC, or Quarter Inch Cartridge.  I'v never heard the term before (naive?).
> I extrapolate that 1/4" is 25/4 = 8.25 mm.  Sounds like 8mm to me.
>    Is it the same?  If not, just what is QIC?  Thanks,  b.

Not even close.  QIC technology is based on cartridges similar to the old 3M
DC300A cartridge, which is over a decade old, although the tape media has
improved in the interim.  The cartridge is about 3" x 6" and has a single drive
wheel which is friction-driven by a rubber-tired wheel in the QIC drive. 
Inside the cartridge, an ingenious drive band turns both the supply and takeup
reels via friction-drive to the outside of the tape packs on the two reels; a
constant-speed rotation of the drive wheel automagically turns the two reels at
the correct (different, and varying) speeds to provide constant tape tension
while maintaining constant tape speed across the head.  There is thus no 
"capstan" and the only thing that touches the recording side of the tape is 
the head.  Upon insertion of the cartridge into the drive, the head pokes 
a bit into the cartridge.  

Contrast with 8mm, which uses an 8mm videotape cartridge and an "extract-and-
thread" technique much like a VCR (which technology it is of course based on);
a loop of tape is pulled from the cartridge and threaded through guide posts,
a capstan, and around a drum.  Here is the most basic difference between 
QIC and 8mm:  The former uses a small number (4?) of linear tracks, while 
8mm uses helical scanning.  

8mm capacity is about 2.2 GB in standard mode, about 5 GB in high-density mode
(comparable, I guess, to "extended play" on a VCR).  Top QIC capacities are, I
believe, around the 500 MB point.  They could be much higher than this -- I
haven't noticed the market for a while.  You will see QIC drives and media
labelled "QIC 120", "QIC 60", etc. -- the number is the capacity in megabytes. 
There are varying degrees of read/write compatibility between drives of various
capacities. 

Several segments of the Unix world have adopted QIC as their standard software
distribution and low-end backup medium.  Neither 8mm nor QIC has been embraced
by DEC in the VMS world, at least not in the U.S.  DEC is selling a SCSI 8mm
drive, the TKZ08, for DECstations (Unix RISC boxes), and rumors are that DEC
Japan will sell you this same drive to go with your VAXstation -- but for some
reason it isn't available for that purpose in the U.S.  (I've often wondered
what would happen if you did connect a TKZ08 to a VAXstation's SCSI port...
since the VAXstation works with 3rd-party 8mm SCSI drives with no problem.)

