>What is a KXT11-CA, board number M8377? It seems to have a T-11 
>processor, but there is a lot of other, strange stuff there.
>Keith Harwood.

It's either a single board computer or a coprocessor, depending which
way you set a 16 position switch located up in a corner.

It probably has 32k or ram. You can add more. It has two or three serial
ports, one of which is very complex...memory is fading here..'complex'
is probably not the best word to use but I seem to remember that this
one port did everything but fix lunch. It's got a parallel port.

If you want to use it as a coprocessor in an RSX or VMS system, you get
the appropriate software package, develop your coprocessor application
and download it into the KXT11. I suggest you use assembley language due
to the memory restrictions.

I've never used it as an SBC so I can't help you there.

Mickey.


