In article <3336309@zl2tnm.gp.co.nz>, don@zl2tnm.gp.co.nz (Don Stokes) writes:
> (a) The BA23's internal I/O bulkhead has two sets of connectors for fixed
> disks.  Is there any magic to getting two drives into the cabinet driven
> by one RQDX3 without resorting to an external box and RQDXE (apart from 
> changing the unit number)?

  The only "supported" configuration for 2 fixed disks in a BA23 is with 2
RD3x type drives _and_ a BA23-UC 6-button front panel upgrade. The RD3x are
required due to the power supply being unable to handle 2 RD5x drives. The
BA23-UC is needed to provide ready and write protect signals for the second
unit.

  You can use an external power supply (a PC clone will work fine) and just
run the cables out the back from the 2nd fixed disk connectors.

> (b) To do the above, the TK50's gotta go.  I've just picked up a scrap
> DECmate III (sans disk drives, monitor & keyboard), partly as spares for
> my working III+ and partly as a potential drive holder (it has space
> for one full height drive).  Can I place the TK50 in the DECmate chassis
> with the existing cabling (ie rerouting the cable from the TQK50 out the
> back instead of into the drive bay of the BA23)?  Will the power
> supplies be fighting each other down the signal lines?  If power is a
> problem, is it going to cause grief if I take power from the BA23's PSU
> via a Y-cable?  If it does work, will things complain if the expansion
> chassis gets powered down/up?  Is there something I can put in to
> prevent such problems?

  Yes, you can remote the TK50 if you want. DEC's -DA boxes just pass the
signals (no power) so power and grounding shouldn't be a problem.

> (c) If a decent sized MFM drive were to unexpectedly turn up on my
> doorstep, does anyone have the good oil on formatting the sucker for use
> with the RQDX3?  Terry?

  Current revs of the RQDX3 don't readily support user-defined disk geometry,
so you're limited to the DEC drive models. I've posted the list in the past,
and can dig it up again if you need it. It's best to use either the PDP-11
formatter or the resident formatter in a VS2000 to format the disk - the VAX
formatter costs way too much.

